<div class="content-stack content-stack-sm">
	<div>
		<h6 class="mb-2"><strong>Black Box</strong></h6>
		<p class="copy mb-0">
			The black box is an armored and isolated low-power recording device, typically positioned within several meters of a functional <?php echo sf_lore_link('switch-gates', 'switch gate'); ?> exchange platform. During typical operations, all official comms chatter, diagnostic reports, security details, personnel logs, and inventory reports are uploaded automatically to the black box. Under normal circumstances the black box is an afterthought, a redundant set-and-forget system that stays out of the way and out of mind. In the event of an emergency, however, such as a lock lost through axiomatic instability or the total loss of a <?php echo sf_lore_link('switch-port', 'switch port'); ?> through natural disaster, violence, or other more esoteric means, this armored little recording box is often the only record of what transpired. As such, black boxes are often locked away or even buried to prevent tampering.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div>
		<h6 class="mb-2"><strong>Turtle Box</strong></h6>
		<p class="copy mb-0">
			Turtle boxes are fist-sized recording devices, immobile and armored against the elements and with shielded circuitry to ward against electromagnetic and ionizing radiation. These devices are deployed early in the process of automated planar exploration to measure radiological signatures, astronomical observations, climate conditions, soil samples, and much more.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div>
		<h6 class="mb-2"><strong>Screamer</strong></h6>
		<p class="copy mb-0">
			Screamers are simple devices, little more than a collection of durable high-bandwidth radio antennas in an armored shell. When a turtle box fails to return, a screamer is sent in its stead, which spends its time in situ taking simple measurements of position, radiological conditions, and temperature while attempting to establish a connection with the lost drone. During the exact moments of the scheduled return exchange, the screamer earns its title by “screaming” a dense and powerful burst of information spiraled across dozens of simultaneous ultra-high-frequency radio channels. Though the gate exchange itself is instantaneous, the radio waves that exist in the exchanged space make it across universes to be recorded by prepared listening devices. The information returned by a screamer is often the final say in whether a plane can be explored at all, and getting no return signal can spell the end of the exploration process.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div>
		<h6 class="mb-2"><strong>Expedition Drone</strong></h6>
		<p class="copy mb-0">
			Expedition drones are a classification of automated survey drones purpose-built for the unique circumstances of the plane for which they’re intended. Often modified in-house or in the field to respond to the emerging circumstances of unexpected paraphysics, these drones serve a vital role in providing expedition teams with the information they’ll need to get the job done and return home safely.
		</p>
	</div>
</div>
